A destroy zeroes the pool it names but not the pools below it,
so their memory goes back to user mode still holding their objects:
other processes' tables, and the registers their threads saved.
Memory crosses zeroed.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: memory left the kernel with bytes not zeroed

diff --git a/kernel/pool.c b/kernel/pool.c
--- a/kernel/pool.c
+++ b/kernel/pool.c
@@ -77,3 +77,3 @@ void pool_destroy(struct pool *pool)
         CALL_WALK(memset);
-        memset(p2v(p->base), 0, p->size);
+        memset(p2v(p->base), 0, p == pool ? p->size : 0);
     }
